\chapter{Jet Dynamics and Poynting Flux}

\section{Overview}
Relativistic jet dynamics arise from the interplay between kinetic energy,
electromagnetic fields, and magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) confinement. This chapter
develops the full theoretical framework governing the formation, acceleration,
and collimation of the Matter Jet Mk-30. Emphasis is placed on Poynting flux
transport, magnetic nozzle geometry, and relativistic stability criteria.

\section{Relativistic Jet Formation}
A relativistic jet is generated when a baryonic mass stream is accelerated to
ultra-relativistic velocities under the influence of strong electromagnetic
fields. The Mk-30 engine achieves this through:
\begin{itemize}
\item baryonic aerosol injection,
\item REBCO superconducting magnetic confinement,
\item relativistic plasma acceleration,
\item Poynting flux conversion.
\end{itemize}

The jet velocity is
\[
v = 0.995c,
\]
yielding a Lorentz factor
\[
\gamma = 10.015.
\]

\section{Momentum Flux and Jet Thrust}
The relativistic momentum flux is
\begin{equation}
\Pi = \gamma^2 \rho v^2,
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ is mass density.

The thrust is
\begin{equation}
F_{\text{jet}} = \gamma \dot{m} v.
\end{equation}

For the Mk-30,
\[
F_{\text{jet}} \approx 38.48~\text{MN}.
\]

\section{Electromagnetic Acceleration}
The electromagnetic force density is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{f}_{\text{EM}} = \mathbf{J} \times \mathbf{B},
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{J}$ is current density and $\mathbf{B}$ is magnetic field.

The Mk-30 nozzle generates
\[
B_z = 1450~\text{T},
\]
producing extreme axial acceleration.

\section{Poynting Flux Transport}
The Poynting vector is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{S} = \frac{1}{\mu_0} \mathbf{E} \times \mathbf{B},
\end{equation}
representing electromagnetic energy flow.

The total Poynting power is
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{Poynting}} = \int_A \mathbf{S} \cdot d\mathbf{A}.
\end{equation}

For the Mk-30,
\[
P_{\text{Poynting}} \approx P_0 = 10^{16}~\text{W}.
\]

\section{Magnetization Parameter}
The magnetization parameter is
\begin{equation}
\sigma = \frac{B^2}{4\pi \gamma \rho c^2}.
\end{equation}

High $\sigma$ indicates magnetic dominance; low $\sigma$ indicates kinetic
dominance.

For the Mk-30:
\[
\sigma_{\text{core}} = 15.2, \qquad
\sigma_{\text{terminal}} = 0.1.
\]

This confirms efficient magnetic-to-kinetic conversion.

\section{Jet Collimation}
The collimation half-angle is
\begin{equation}
\theta_{\text{open}} \approx \frac{1}{\gamma}.
\end{equation}

For $\gamma = 10.015$,
\[
\theta_{\text{open}} \approx 5.72^\circ.
\]

This narrow angle is characteristic of relativistic jets.

\section{Magnetic Nozzle Geometry}
The nozzle geometry is defined by:
\begin{itemize}
\item axial field $B_z$,
\item radial gradient $\partial B / \partial r$,
\item nozzle throat radius $r_0$,
\item confinement length $L$.
\end{itemize}

The Mk-30 nozzle achieves:
\[
\frac{\partial B}{\partial r} = 8.12 \times 10^3~\text{T/m}.
\]

This gradient produces strong radial confinement.

\section{Relativistic Stability Criteria}
Jet stability requires suppression of:
\begin{itemize}
\item Rayleigh-Taylor instabilities,
\item Kelvin-Helmholtz shear instabilities,
\item kink modes ($m = 1, 2$).
\end{itemize}

The Alfvén speed is
\begin{equation}
v_A = \frac{B}{\sqrt{\mu_0 \rho}},
\end{equation}
which approaches
\[
v_A \approx 0.98c
\]
in the Mk-30 core.

Kink damping occurs within
\[
\tau < 0.12~\mu\text{s}.
\]

\section{Energy Conversion Efficiency}
The Mk-30 achieves
\[
\eta = 0.95,
\]
meaning 95\% of electromagnetic energy is converted into kinetic jet energy.

The remaining 5\% is dissipated as thermal radiation and removed by GaIn
micro-channel cooling.

\section{Summary}
This chapter develops the full relativistic jet dynamics and Poynting flux
framework underlying the Matter Jet Mk-30. The system exhibits:
\begin{itemize}
\item strong magnetic confinement,
\item efficient electromagnetic acceleration,
\item narrow collimation angle,
\item high magnetization-to-kinetic conversion,
\item relativistic stability.
\end{itemize}

These results form the theoretical basis for the hardware architecture presented
in Chapter 4.

